    {
      "id": "ABmU6vLz104E_C10xVckh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5341.281280378245,
      "y": -2207.565440913957,
      "width": 1617.638916015625,
      "height": 10400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bAV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 212645242,
      "version": 66,
      "versionNonce": 376199290,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787590715550,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "In Apache Kafka, a **partition is a smaller ordered log inside a topic**.\n\nThink of a Kafka topic as being split into several independent lanes:\n\n```text\nTopic: orders\n\nPartition 0\n[order-1] -> [order-4] -> [order-7]\n\nPartition 1\n[order-2] -> [order-5] -> [order-8]\n\nPartition 2\n[order-3] -> [order-6] -> [order-9]\n```\n\n### Why partitions exist\n\nPartitions allow Kafka to **scale horizontally and process messages in parallel**.\n\nIf `orders` had only one partition:\n\n```text\norders\n   |\n   v\nPartition 0\n[A] -> [B] -> [C] -> [D] -> [E]\n             |\n         1 consumer\n```\n\nOnly **one consumer in the same consumer group** can actively process that partition.\n\nBut with 3 partitions:\n\n```text\nTopic: orders\n\nPartition 0 ---> Consumer 1\nPartition 1 ---> Consumer 2\nPartition 2 ---> Consumer 3\n```\n\nNow Kafka can process 3 streams simultaneously.\n\n## The most important rule: ordering\n\nKafka guarantees message ordering **inside a partition**, not across the whole topic.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPartition 0:\nA -> B -> C\n\nPartition 1:\nX -> Y -> Z\n```\n\nKafka guarantees:\n\n```text\nA before B before C\nX before Y before Z\n```\n\nBut it does **not** guarantee:\n\n```text\nA -> X -> B -> Y\n```\n\nacross partitions.\n\n---\n\n## How does Kafka choose a partition?\n\nUsually there are two important cases.\n\n### 1. Message with a key\n\nSuppose you send:\n\n```text\nkey = customer-123\nvalue = order-created\n```\n\nKafka hashes the key:\n\n```text\nhash(customer-123)\n       |\n       v\n   Partition 2\n```\n\nMessages with the same key will normally go to the same partition.\n\n```text\ncustomer-123 -> Partition 2\ncustomer-123 -> Partition 2\ncustomer-123 -> Partition 2\n```\n\nThis is extremely useful.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nTopic: bank-transactions\n```\n\nYou could use:\n\n```text\nkey = accountId\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```text\nAccount 100\n  deposit $100\n  withdraw $20\n  deposit $50\n\n        |\n        v\n\nPartition 1\n[deposit 100] -> [withdraw 20] -> [deposit 50]\n```\n\nBecause they stay in the same partition, their order is preserved.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Message without a key\n\nIf there is no key, the Kafka producer distributes records across partitions according to its partitioning strategy, generally trying to distribute load efficiently.\n\n```text\nProducer\n   |\n   +----> Partition 0\n   |\n   +----> Partition 1\n   |\n   +----> Partition 2\n```\n\n---\n\n# Partitions and Consumer Groups\n\nThis relationship is very important in Kafka.\n\nImagine:\n\n```text\nTopic: orders\nPartitions: 3\n```\n\nand:\n\n```text\nConsumer Group: payment-service\n```\n\nWith one consumer:\n\n```text\n              Consumer 1\n             /    |    \\\n            /     |     \\\nPartition 0   Partition 1   Partition 2\n```\n\nWith three consumers:\n\n```text\nPartition 0 ---> Consumer 1\nPartition 1 ---> Consumer 2\nPartition 2 ---> Consumer 3\n```\n\nGreat parallelism.\n\nBut if you create **4 consumers**:\n\n```text\nPartition 0 ---> Consumer 1\nPartition 1 ---> Consumer 2\nPartition 2 ---> Consumer 3\n\n                Consumer 4\n                  IDLE\n```\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause **one partition can only be consumed by one consumer inside the same consumer group at a time**.\n\nSo:\n\n```text\nMaximum useful consumers\n        ≈\nNumber of partitions\n```\n\nfor that consumer group.\n\n---\n\n# Partition vs Broker\n\nA partition is **not a server**.\n\nA Kafka broker is a Kafka server.\n\nPartitions are distributed between brokers.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nTopic: orders\n6 partitions\n\nKafka Cluster\n\nBroker 1\n ├─ Partition 0\n └─ Partition 3\n\nBroker 2\n ├─ Partition 1\n └─ Partition 4\n\nBroker 3\n ├─ Partition 2\n └─ Partition 5\n```\n\nThis is one of the reasons Kafka scales so well.\n\nMore brokers + appropriately partitioned topics can distribute storage and processing.\n\n---\n\n# Partitions also have replicas\n\nIn production, you normally don't want a partition to exist on only one broker.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPartition 0\n\nBroker 1\nLeader\n\nBroker 2\nReplica\n\nBroker 3\nReplica\n```\n\nThe producer and consumers normally interact with the **leader**.\n\n```text\nProducer\n   |\n   v\nBroker 1\nPartition 0 Leader\n   |\n   +------> Broker 2 Replica\n   |\n   +------> Broker 3 Replica\n```\n\nIf Broker 1 fails, an eligible replica can become the new leader.\n\nThis is how Kafka combines:\n\n```text\nPartitions → scalability\nReplicas   → fault tolerance\n```\n\n---\n\n## A practical Java backend example\n\nImagine an e-commerce architecture:\n\n```text\nOrder Service\n     |\n     | OrderCreated\n     v\nKafka Topic\norders\n     |\n     ├── Partition 0\n     ├── Partition 1\n     ├── Partition 2\n     └── Partition 3\n             |\n             v\n      Payment Service\n      Consumer Group\n```\n\nYou could publish using:\n\n```java\nkafkaTemplate.send(\n    \"orders\",\n    order.getCustomerId(),\n    order\n);\n```\n\nHere:\n\n```text\nTopic = orders\n\nKey   = customerId\n\nValue = order\n```\n\nKafka conceptually does:\n\n```text\nhash(customerId)\n       |\n       v\npartition\n```\n\nTherefore orders for the same customer tend to remain ordered:\n\n```text\ncustomer-42\n\nOrderCreated\n     ↓\nOrderPaid\n     ↓\nOrderShipped\n\nall → Partition 2\n```\n\n---\n\n## The mental model to remember\n\n```text\nKafka\n |\n +-- Topic\n      |\n      +-- Partition 0\n      |    [0][1][2][3][4]\n      |\n      +-- Partition 1\n      |    [0][1][2][3]\n      |\n      +-- Partition 2\n           [0][1][2][3][4][5]\n```\n\nEvery message in a partition gets an **offset**:\n\n```text\nPartition 0\n\nOffset:\n  0       1       2       3\n  |       |       |       |\n [A] --> [B] --> [C] --> [D]\n```\n\nSo a very useful summary is:\n\n```text\nTOPIC\n  ↓\ndivided into\n  ↓\nPARTITIONS\n  ↓\neach partition is an ordered log\n  ↓\nmessages receive OFFSETS\n  ↓\npartitions enable PARALLELISM\n  ↓\nsame KEY → same partition\n  ↓\nsame partition → ordering preserved\n```\n\n**In one sentence:** a Kafka partition is an **ordered, append-only piece of a topic that lets Kafka distribute data and processing across brokers and consumers.**\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "In Apache Kafka, a **partition is a smaller ordered log inside a topic**.\n\nThink of a Kafka topic as being split into several independent lanes:\n\n```text\nTopic: orders\n\nPartition 0\n[order-1] -> [order-4] -> [order-7]\n\nPartition 1\n[order-2] -> [order-5] -> [order-8]\n\nPartition 2\n[order-3] -> [order-6] -> [order-9]\n```\n\n### Why partitions exist\n\nPartitions allow Kafka to **scale horizontally and process messages in parallel**.\n\nIf `orders` had only one partition:\n\n```text\norders\n   |\n   v\nPartition 0\n[A] -> [B] -> [C] -> [D] -> [E]\n             |\n         1 consumer\n```\n\nOnly **one consumer in the same consumer group** can actively process that partition.\n\nBut with 3 partitions:\n\n```text\nTopic: orders\n\nPartition 0 ---> Consumer 1\nPartition 1 ---> Consumer 2\nPartition 2 ---> Consumer 3\n```\n\nNow Kafka can process 3 streams simultaneously.\n\n## The most important rule: ordering\n\nKafka guarantees message ordering **inside a partition**, not across the whole topic.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPartition 0:\nA -> B -> C\n\nPartition 1:\nX -> Y -> Z\n```\n\nKafka guarantees:\n\n```text\nA before B before C\nX before Y before Z\n```\n\nBut it does **not** guarantee:\n\n```text\nA -> X -> B -> Y\n```\n\nacross partitions.\n\n---\n\n## How does Kafka choose a partition?\n\nUsually there are two important cases.\n\n### 1. Message with a key\n\nSuppose you send:\n\n```text\nkey = customer-123\nvalue = order-created\n```\n\nKafka hashes the key:\n\n```text\nhash(customer-123)\n       |\n       v\n   Partition 2\n```\n\nMessages with the same key will normally go to the same partition.\n\n```text\ncustomer-123 -> Partition 2\ncustomer-123 -> Partition 2\ncustomer-123 -> Partition 2\n```\n\nThis is extremely useful.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nTopic: bank-transactions\n```\n\nYou could use:\n\n```text\nkey = accountId\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```text\nAccount 100\n  deposit $100\n  withdraw $20\n  deposit $50\n\n        |\n        v\n\nPartition 1\n[deposit 100] -> [withdraw 20] -> [deposit 50]\n```\n\nBecause they stay in the same partition, their order is preserved.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Message without a key\n\nIf there is no key, the Kafka producer distributes records across partitions according to its partitioning strategy, generally trying to distribute load efficiently.\n\n```text\nProducer\n   |\n   +----> Partition 0\n   |\n   +----> Partition 1\n   |\n   +----> Partition 2\n```\n\n---\n\n# Partitions and Consumer Groups\n\nThis relationship is very important in Kafka.\n\nImagine:\n\n```text\nTopic: orders\nPartitions: 3\n```\n\nand:\n\n```text\nConsumer Group: payment-service\n```\n\nWith one consumer:\n\n```text\n              Consumer 1\n             /    |    \\\n            /     |     \\\nPartition 0   Partition 1   Partition 2\n```\n\nWith three consumers:\n\n```text\nPartition 0 ---> Consumer 1\nPartition 1 ---> Consumer 2\nPartition 2 ---> Consumer 3\n```\n\nGreat parallelism.\n\nBut if you create **4 consumers**:\n\n```text\nPartition 0 ---> Consumer 1\nPartition 1 ---> Consumer 2\nPartition 2 ---> Consumer 3\n\n                Consumer 4\n                  IDLE\n```\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause **one partition can only be consumed by one consumer inside the same consumer group at a time**.\n\nSo:\n\n```text\nMaximum useful consumers\n        ≈\nNumber of partitions\n```\n\nfor that consumer group.\n\n---\n\n# Partition vs Broker\n\nA partition is **not a server**.\n\nA Kafka broker is a Kafka server.\n\nPartitions are distributed between brokers.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nTopic: orders\n6 partitions\n\nKafka Cluster\n\nBroker 1\n ├─ Partition 0\n └─ Partition 3\n\nBroker 2\n ├─ Partition 1\n └─ Partition 4\n\nBroker 3\n ├─ Partition 2\n └─ Partition 5\n```\n\nThis is one of the reasons Kafka scales so well.\n\nMore brokers + appropriately partitioned topics can distribute storage and processing.\n\n---\n\n# Partitions also have replicas\n\nIn production, you normally don't want a partition to exist on only one broker.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPartition 0\n\nBroker 1\nLeader\n\nBroker 2\nReplica\n\nBroker 3\nReplica\n```\n\nThe producer and consumers normally interact with the **leader**.\n\n```text\nProducer\n   |\n   v\nBroker 1\nPartition 0 Leader\n   |\n   +------> Broker 2 Replica\n   |\n   +------> Broker 3 Replica\n```\n\nIf Broker 1 fails, an eligible replica can become the new leader.\n\nThis is how Kafka combines:\n\n```text\nPartitions → scalability\nReplicas   → fault tolerance\n```\n\n---\n\n## A practical Java backend example\n\nImagine an e-commerce architecture:\n\n```text\nOrder Service\n     |\n     | OrderCreated\n     v\nKafka Topic\norders\n     |\n     ├── Partition 0\n     ├── Partition 1\n     ├── Partition 2\n     └── Partition 3\n             |\n             v\n      Payment Service\n      Consumer Group\n```\n\nYou could publish using:\n\n```java\nkafkaTemplate.send(\n    \"orders\",\n    order.getCustomerId(),\n    order\n);\n```\n\nHere:\n\n```text\nTopic = orders\n\nKey   = customerId\n\nValue = order\n```\n\nKafka conceptually does:\n\n```text\nhash(customerId)\n       |\n       v\npartition\n```\n\nTherefore orders for the same customer tend to remain ordered:\n\n```text\ncustomer-42\n\nOrderCreated\n     ↓\nOrderPaid\n     ↓\nOrderShipped\n\nall → Partition 2\n```\n\n---\n\n## The mental model to remember\n\n```text\nKafka\n |\n +-- Topic\n      |\n      +-- Partition 0\n      |    [0][1][2][3][4]\n      |\n      +-- Partition 1\n      |    [0][1][2][3]\n      |\n      +-- Partition 2\n           [0][1][2][3][4][5]\n```\n\nEvery message in a partition gets an **offset**:\n\n```text\nPartition 0\n\nOffset:\n  0       1       2       3\n  |       |       |       |\n [A] --> [B] --> [C] --> [D]\n```\n\nSo a very useful summary is:\n\n```text\nTOPIC\n  ↓\ndivided into\n  ↓\nPARTITIONS\n  ↓\neach partition is an ordered log\n  ↓\nmessages receive OFFSETS\n  ↓\npartitions enable PARALLELISM\n  ↓\nsame KEY → same partition\n  ↓\nsame partition → ordering preserved\n```\n\n**In one sentence:** a Kafka partition is an **ordered, append-only piece of a topic that lets Kafka distribute data and processing across brokers and consumers.**\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
